 \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe height of a cylindrical pole is 12 feet and its circumference is 2 feet. A rope is attached to a point on the circumference at the bottom of the pole. The rope is then wrapped tightly around the pole four times before it reaches a point on the top directly above the starting point at the bottom. What is the minimum number of feet in the length of the rope? Express your answer in simplest radical form.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"91767310fcec353077102bd69afb978f225170b1b0ce8f070438a5038d74e1f3"},"seconds":7.8320152759552,"seed":769487381824842931,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to determine the length of the rope that wraps around the cylindrical pole four times and reaches the top of the pole directly above the starting point. We can visualize the problem by \"unrolling\" the cylinder into a rectangle.\n\n1. **Unroll the cylinder**: When we unroll the cylinder, the height of the rectangle is the height of the pole, which is 12 feet, and the width of the rectangle is the circumference of the pole, which is 2 feet. The rope, when unrolled, forms a straight line on this rectangle.\n\n2. **Determine the path of the rope**: The rope wraps around the pole four times, so it covers a width of \\(4 \\times 2 = 8\\) feet on the unrolled rectangle. The height of the rectangle remains 12 feet.\n\n3. **Use the Pythagorean theorem**: The length of the rope is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of 12 feet and 8 feet. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the rope} = \\sqrt{12^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 64} = \\sqrt{208} = \\sqrt{16 \\times 13} = 4\\sqrt{13}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum number of feet in the length of the rope is \\(\\boxed{4\\sqrt{13}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4\\sqrt{13}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:419"}
